Kairo Tests
===========

Run everything from the repo root:

    lit Tests -s                    (or build/llvm/bin/llvm-lit Tests -s)

No flags or environment needed, on any host. Any `lit` works: pip's, the
distro's (lit 23 deprecated the external shell this suite uses; lit.cfg.py
forces it), or the one in build/llvm. FileCheck, clang and the clang resource
dir default to the toolchain staged beside the compiler (Scripts/
stage_tree.py), then build/llvm, then PATH. builtin and std are always the
source tree's Lib/. No --sysroot: the compiler picks the sysroot bundled for
its default target, as a bare `kairo` does. Every default can be overridden:
--param filecheck= / clang= (or CLANG_BIN) / resource_dir= / sysroot=.

Add `-v` to see the output of failing tests, or `-a` to see it for every test.


WHICH COMPILER GETS TESTED

These test STAGE 1, the self-hosted compiler built from Compiler/**/*.k -- the
thing you are editing. lit resolves it in this order:

    --param kairo=/abs/path        explicit, always wins
    KAIRO_BIN=/abs/path            environment
    build/<triple>/{debug,release}/bin/kairo, whichever was built LAST

lit prints the binary it picked (`note: kairo: ...`). The newest build is
the one you just made, so the edit loop (`kbld kairo --debug`) needs no
KAIRO_BIN. An older binary as an A/B baseline (a release build from before
your change) has to be named explicitly:

    KAIRO_BIN=$PWD/build/<triple>/release/bin/kairo lit Tests -s

lit deliberately REFUSES to fall back to PATH. A bare `kairo` on PATH is stage
0, the prebuilt C++ transpiler that acts as the build driver -- it will answer,
lack the flags these tests use, and quietly test the wrong compiler. A missing
binary is a loud error here rather than a silent pass.


READING THE RESULTS

The suite does not currently go green, and is not expected to. A fresh debug
build of the current tree gives:

    Total Discovered Tests: 250
      Passed           : 118
      Expectedly Failed:   1
      Unresolved       :  77
      Failed           :  54

A bare `lit Tests` against the stale release binary gives 107 passed / 65 failed
instead. Neither number is wrong; they are different compilers. Always know
which one you ran before quoting a figure.

The 54 failures are unimplemented language features -- alignof, closures,
f-string interpolation and similar -- not regressions. Before concluding that a
change broke something, get the failure LIST before and after and diff them; the
count alone is not informative, and neither is any individual failure without
that baseline.

All 77 UNRESOLVED are files with no RUN line: support files that other tests
import, and fixtures never converted to lit. lit reports them rather than
skipping them. They are noise, not breakage.


LAYOUT

The suite mirrors the compiler pipeline. PascalCase directories are
categories; a lowercase directory is ONE multi-file fixture (a test plus the
files it imports) or a documented role (parity/, kairo-only/).

    Preprocessor/       macro expansion, import scanning
      Errors/           preprocessor diagnostics
    Parser/
      Expr/             expression parsing, one construct per file
      Stmt/             statement parsing, one file per Stmt kind
        CompoundStmt/
      Grammar/          declaration-level grammar rules
      Disambiguation/   generic-vs-comparison and dependent-chain disambiguation
      Modes/            parameter/binding modes in the grammar
      Integration/      larger mixed-construct parser runs
    Sema/
      Lookup/           name resolution (phase N), qualified + member lookup
      Types/            type resolution (phase T), aliases, cycles, lang items
      Imports/          imports, the prelude and the std / cxx overlays
        per_form/         one test per import form over a shared corpus
        imports_all_forms/  every form in one TU (IMPORTS.md 1-9)
        emit_plan/        which TUs get emitted (IMPORTS.md 11)
        module_roots/     a pinned library beside the cwd, reached by name only (also via -I); `-I name=dir` is an error
        module_pins/      libraries named by a module.k pin (`module geo`); a pin outside module.k is an error
        module_pins_dup/  two pins with one name (R018E); its own cwd
        search_dirs/      unnamed -I dirs: relative module paths; F20 (no R018E)
        search_dirs_dup/  one module path from two search dirs (R017E at an import, R018E as two inputs); its own cwd
        Inputs/           corpora of the single-file tests beside it: module_walkup (a library found walking up
                          from the file), module_pins_deep / _late / _symlink, std_dash_i
      ExprTyper/        expression typing, overloads, call arguments
      Check/            post-resolution checks (lifecycle, C++ name clashes)
      Operators/        operator declaration / use rules
      Panic/            panic effects: sets, unhandled calls, try/catch arms (PANIC.md)
        Inputs/cross_tu/  a library whose sets are inferred, one that stops early, an importer
      Modes/            argument modes; kairo-only/ has no C++ oracle
      FFI/              C++ interop; parity/ is differential against clang
        Builtins/         clang builtins as unqualified names (RESOLUTION.md 2.5)
      RedeclMerge/      redeclaration merging; parity/ + kairo-only/, see README
      Scenarios/        numbered end-to-end Sema walkthroughs (+ runner script)
    Codegen/            code emission and link-and-run tests
    Driver/             driver-level behaviour (--explain, --print-*, bundled sysroot)
    CLI/                one test per command-line flag, pinning what a LIVE flag
                        does (mostly via --print-clang-args / --print-ld-args).
                        Flags that are accepted but ignored have no test here on
                        purpose; see HELP_AUDIT.md at the repo root.
    Bugs/
      Snapshots/        characterization snapshots of reported bugs (lit-run;
                        they pin today's output, they do not specify it)
      Triage/           bug-reproducer corpus, EXCLUDED from lit in lit.cfg.py
      KNOWN_BUGS.md

NOT lit suites -- lit never collects them, and they are not part of the test
tree's categories:

    Clang/        proof / oracle material for the clang interop (the path is
                  hardcoded in Compiler/Interop/Clang/ClangLocMapTest.k)
    Manual/       run by hand, not by lit; personal scratch

    NumericConformance/
                  Self-checking Kairo programs that assert their
                  own arithmetic and report via exit code, so they stay valid
                  the day stage 1 can emit a binary. A lit.local.cfg keeps lit
                  from collecting them. Run with:

                      ./Tests/NumericConformance/run.sh <path-to-kairo>

                  See Tests/NumericConformance/README -- it explains what those
                  programs cover and why numeric bugs get their own directory.

Suite infrastructure lives at the top: lit.cfg.py, gen_checks.py (regenerates
BEGIN/END-GENERATED-CHECKS blocks) and parity_check.py (the %parity driver).


ADDING A TEST

Copy the RUN lines from a neighbouring file in the same directory, or let
gen_checks.py write them from the compiler's current output:

    python3 Tests/gen_checks.py --mode=diag,sema Tests/Sema/Check/foo.k
    python3 Tests/gen_checks.py --mode=cxx --focus=Grid,probe Tests/Codegen/x.k
    python3 Tests/gen_checks.py --mode=run Tests/Codegen/x.k
    python3 Tests/gen_checks.py Tests/Sema/Check/foo.k        # regenerate
    python3 Tests/gen_checks.py --check Tests/Codegen/**/*.k  # diff only

Modes: ast (--print-ast=tree), diag (every diagnostic, note and fix-it, plus
--implicit-check-not so a new one fails), sema / lowered (named sections of the
test's own TU), cxx (the test's own C++, shared runtime boilerplate dropped),
run (compile, link, run: exit code and stdout). Generated output still has to
be READ: it records what the compiler does today, not what it should do.

`%kairo` expands to the resolved binary plus `--error-format=basic` and the
--builtins-dir/--std-dir/--resource-dir roots, `%s` to the test file, `%t` to
a scratch path.

Tests whose expected behaviour is not implemented yet get `// XFAIL: *` and a
comment saying what the CHECK lines describe and when to remove the marker --
see Sema/Types/type_cycle_generic.k for the pattern. An
XFAIL that starts XPASSing means the feature landed.
